{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-118755" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is convergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-118755" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution.  Let $u={x}^{2}+4$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4))|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty $, we have that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)\\to \\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4))|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{2}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty $, we have that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)\\to \\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement III is true. The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nIn conclusion,  only statements II and III are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-118755" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8662d0bc6c05c63a25a6609f0e573379fdb905db9d732128a76509fc9d5b63a6"} {"question" "q-118755" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-118755" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-118755" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-81375" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is convergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-81375" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution.  Let $u={x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{e}^{-u}+C \\\\  & =-{e}^{-{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({-{e}^{-{x}^{2}}|}_{0}^{a}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-{e}^{-{a}^{2}}+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the integral converges.\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({-{e}^{-{x}^{2}}|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-{e}^{0}+{e}^{-{a}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-1+0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the integral converges.\n\n- Statement III is false. The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1-1=0.\n$$\n\nSo the integral converges.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-81375" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8662d0bc6c05c63a25a6609f0e573379fdb905db9d732128a76509fc9d5b63a6"} {"question" "q-81375" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-81375" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-81375" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-51075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=3{x}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is convergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-51075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nLet's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({x}^{3}{|}_{0}^{a}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({a}^{3}-{0}^{3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{a}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty $, we have that ${a}^{3}\\to \\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({x}^{3}{|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({0}^{3}-{a}^{3}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{a}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty $, we have that ${a}^{3}\\to -\\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement II is true. The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nIn conclusion,  only statements II and III are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-51075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8662d0bc6c05c63a25a6609f0e573379fdb905db9d732128a76509fc9d5b63a6"} {"question" "q-51075" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-51075" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-51075" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-118761" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is convergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-118761" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution.  Let $u={x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}))|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty $, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}))|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty $, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement III is true. The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-118761" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8662d0bc6c05c63a25a6609f0e573379fdb905db9d732128a76509fc9d5b63a6"} {"question" "q-118761" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-118761" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-118761" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-20788" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi x+1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is convergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-20788" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nLet's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{5\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi x+1){|}_{0}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi a+1)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡1].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty $, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi a+1)$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{5\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi x+1){|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\pi }{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi x+1)].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty $, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi x+1)$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement III is true. The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nIn conclusion,  only statements II and III are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-20788" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8662d0bc6c05c63a25a6609f0e573379fdb905db9d732128a76509fc9d5b63a6"} {"question" "q-20788" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-20788" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-20788" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-20550" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }4{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-20550" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }4{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }4{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}4{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }4{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let $u={x}^{4}$,  then $\\text{d}u=4{x}^{3}\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{e}^{-u}+C \\\\  & =-{e}^{-{x}^{4}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}4{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}4{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-{e}^{-{x}^{4}}{|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{0}-{e}^{-{a}^{4}}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{e}^{-{a}^{4}}) \\\\  & =-1+0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **odd** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }4{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }4{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}4{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }4{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-20550" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8662d0bc6c05c63a25a6609f0e573379fdb905db9d732128a76509fc9d5b63a6"} {"question" "q-20550" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-20550" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["text" "The integral is divergent"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-20550" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-351545" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-351545" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3})|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2a}{3})] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot [0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-351545" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8662d0bc6c05c63a25a6609f0e573379fdb905db9d732128a76509fc9d5b63a6"} {"question" "q-351545" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-351545" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-351585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-351585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u=2{x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=4x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (-6x){e}^{-2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-u}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-2{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}(-6x){e}^{-2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}(-6x){e}^{-2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-2{x}^{2}})|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{0}-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-2{a}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{6|x|}{{e}^{2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-351585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8662d0bc6c05c63a25a6609f0e573379fdb905db9d732128a76509fc9d5b63a6"} {"question" "q-351585" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-351585" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-118774" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-118774" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u={x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{e}^{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{e}^{-u}+C \\\\  & =-{e}^{-{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(-{e}^{-{x}^{2}})|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-{e}^{0}-(-{e}^{-{a}^{2}})) \\\\  & =-1+0 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **odd** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-118774" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8662d0bc6c05c63a25a6609f0e573379fdb905db9d732128a76509fc9d5b63a6"} {"question" "q-118774" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-118774" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["text" "The integral diverges"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-118774" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-347621" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-347621" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u=5+{x}^{8}$,  then $\\text{d}u=8{x}^{7}\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{u}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5+{x}^{8}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(-\\frac{1}{5+{x}^{8}})|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{5+0}-\\frac{1}{5+{a}^{8}}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{5+{a}^{8}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **odd** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-347621" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8662d0bc6c05c63a25a6609f0e573379fdb905db9d732128a76509fc9d5b63a6"} {"question" "q-347621" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-347621" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-351595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/assets/ccd0312a25669fec949475eaadb042e0c660d094c7579f5bc4f98b68fc9be78d.png)\n\nFind the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-351595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}){|}_{a}^{0}\\,) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}(0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3\\pi }{16}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{16}+\\frac{3\\pi }{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-351595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8662d0bc6c05c63a25a6609f0e573379fdb905db9d732128a76509fc9d5b63a6"} {"question" "q-351595" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-351595" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-81168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/assets/478ea8a880f959248ae2885776ca3c2b3774935f96ddb64ce531adc6fc5b3ef7.png)\n\nFind the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-81168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{3}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{x}{3}|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{a}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{a}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-81168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8662d0bc6c05c63a25a6609f0e573379fdb905db9d732128a76509fc9d5b63a6"} {"question" "q-81168" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-81168" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{6}"] ["math" "\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-81168" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi }{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-351600" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the total area $A$ of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}$ and the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-351600" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's sketch a graph of the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/assets/c2104caf050dd3bb795f29b40ce3047e7c8cea98f7c0a6abede5f92c23a06815.png)\n\nWe find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u={e}^{2x}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{1+({e}^{2x})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{4}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{2x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{2x}){|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{0}-4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{2a}) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-0 \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{4x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi +\\pi  \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-351600" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8662d0bc6c05c63a25a6609f0e573379fdb905db9d732128a76509fc9d5b63a6"} {"question" "q-351600" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-351600" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-6302" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}$ and the $x$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-6302" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u={e}^{x}$,  then $\\text{d}u={e}^{x}\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{x}){|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{0}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function.  Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{-2x}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{-x}}{{e}^{2x}\\cdot ({e}^{-2x}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-6302" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8662d0bc6c05c63a25a6609f0e573379fdb905db9d732128a76509fc9d5b63a6"} {"question" "q-6302" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-6302" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi "] ["math" "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-6302" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-118860" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}$ and the $x$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-118860" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{4}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{x}{4}){|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{a}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}(0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}+\\frac{3\\pi }{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-118860" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8662d0bc6c05c63a25a6609f0e573379fdb905db9d732128a76509fc9d5b63a6"} {"question" "q-118860" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-118860" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{3\\pi }{8}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "q-118860" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/state.json" "file_sha256" "69ed65c6497baf8ccf6da43d097b64599beee2f94070f6519d7b534e5ee68195"} {"question" "e-515" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x) = e^{-x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty f(x) \\, \\textrm dx$ is convergent\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^0 f(x) \\, \\textrm dx$ is divergent\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty f(x) \\, \\textrm dx$ is divergent" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/example-515.json" "file_sha256" "ebe6cc022e3e3ce66000c82fe15fe553fba776051f4315587f5810d534392ff3"} {"question" "e-515" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty, \\infty),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x + \\int_{0}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our integrals.\n\n- Computing the integral in statement I, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n \\int_{0}^{\\infty} e^{-x}\\, \\textrm{d}x\n&=  \\lim_{a \\to \\infty} \\int_{0}^{a} e^{-x}\\, \\textrm{d}x \\\n&=  \\lim_{a \\to \\infty} \\left[-e^{-x}\\right]_0^a\\\n&=  -\\lim_{a \\to \\infty}  \\left({e^{-a}}-1\\right)\\\n&=  1.\n}\n$$\n\nSo the integral converges, and statement I is true.\n\n- Computing the integral in statement II, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{e}^{-x}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{-a}-1) \\\\  & =+\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the integral diverges, and statement II is true.\n\n- The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty f(x) \\, \\textrm dx= \\int_{-\\infty}^0 f(x) \\, \\textrm dx + \\int_0^\\infty f(x) \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$\n\nHowever, the first integral on the right-hand side is divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent, and statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, statements I, II, and III are all true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/example-515.json" "file_sha256" "ebe6cc022e3e3ce66000c82fe15fe553fba776051f4315587f5810d534392ff3"} {"question" "e-514" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate  $\\displaystyle  \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/example-514.json" "file_sha256" "cf713ee822555d2782f2c166d308c1a683c559e0dabb0bfb9ab1a58d9c79ed1a"} {"question" "e-514" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x + \\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t \\int_{-\\infty}^0 \\dfrac {1}{x^2+1} \\,\\textrm{d}x  \n\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t&= \\lim_{a \\to -\\infty} \\int_{a}^0 \\dfrac {1}{x^2+1} \\,\\textrm{d}x\\[5pt]\t\n\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t& =  \\lim_{a \\to -\\infty}\\left( \\arctan (x) \\Big|_a^0 \\right)  \\[5pt]\n\n \t \t\t\t& =  \\lim_{a \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan \\left( 0 \\right) -  \\arctan \\left( a \\right) \\right] \\[5pt]\n\n \t\t\t        & =  0 - \\lim_{a \\to -\\infty}  \\arctan \\left( a \\right) \\[5pt]\n\n                                &= - \\left(-\\dfrac \\pi 2 \\right)\\[5pt]\n\n \t\t\t\t& = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n\t}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function (i.e., it is symmetrical about the $y$-axis). This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^\\infty \\dfrac {1}{x^2+1} \\,\\textrm{d}x  =\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x &=   \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x +  \\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}+ \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\pi.}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/example-514.json" "file_sha256" "cf713ee822555d2782f2c166d308c1a683c559e0dabb0bfb9ab1a58d9c79ed1a"} {"question" "e-516" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y = \\dfrac{9}{1+4x^2}$ and the $x$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/example-516.json" "file_sha256" "471b616119a2ac511ed97549211c2ef010257e21972a7d5915af78dbbce75f4d"} {"question" "e-516" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's sketch a graph of the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/assets/6c86b60d83c741287c2b7770bfddd67fe9dcfece5ba255edf8ebdb8f0360a975.png)\n\nIn this case, we must calculate the improper integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle  \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{9}{4x^2+1}\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nAs usual, we break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle  \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{9}{4x^2+1}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\displaystyle  \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} \\dfrac{9}{4x^2+1}\\,\\textrm{d}x  + \\displaystyle  \\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{9}{4x^2+1}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t \\int_{-\\infty}^0 \\dfrac {9}{4x^2+1} \\,\\textrm{d}x  & =  9\\lim_{a \\to -\\infty}\\left( \\left[\\dfrac{1}{2}\\arctan \\left(2x \\right) \\right]_a^0 \\right)  \\[5pt]\n\n \t \t\t\t                                                   & = \\dfrac{9}{2} \\lim_{a \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan \\left( 0 \\right) -  \\arctan \\left( 2a \\right) \\right]  \\[5pt] \n\n \t\t\t                                                           & =  0 - \\dfrac{9}{2}\\lim_{a \\to -\\infty}  \\arctan \\left( 2a \\right) \\[5pt] \n\n \t\t\t                                                           & = -\\dfrac{9}{2} \\left(-\\dfrac \\pi 2 \\right)\\[5pt]\n\n \t\t\t\t                                                   & = \\dfrac{9\\pi}{4}.\n\t}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. Therefore, we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^\\infty\\dfrac {9}{4x^2+1} \\,\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{9\\pi}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{9}{4x^2+1}\\,\\textrm{d}x =  \\dfrac{9\\pi}{4}+ \\dfrac{9\\pi}{4}= \\dfrac{9\\pi}{2} .\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the required area is equal to $\\dfrac{9\\pi}{2}$ square units." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041037/example-516.json" "file_sha256" "471b616119a2ac511ed97549211c2ef010257e21972a7d5915af78dbbce75f4d"}] "usage" {"e-516" [] "e-515" [] "q-81168" [] "q-51075" [] "e-514" []} "basis" 1475 "retractions" [[17592186106306 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = 3x^{2}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty}f(x)dx$ is convergent\n2. $\\displaystyle \\int_{- \\infty}^{0}f(x)dx$ is divergent\n3. $\\displaystyle \\int_{- \\infty}^{\\infty}f(x)dx$ is divergent"] [17592186106421 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1382/Source/Images/q-81168.png)\n\nFind the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y = \\frac{1}{9 + x^{2}}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above."] [17592186105531 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = e^{-x}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty f(x) \\, \\textrm dx$ is convergent\n2. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^0 f(x) \\, \\textrm dx$ is divergent\n3. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty f(x) \\, \\textrm dx$ is divergent"] [17592186105531 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty, \\infty)$, then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x + \\int_{0}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results.\n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our integrals.\n\n- Computing the integral in statement I, we get\n$\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned} \\int_{0}^{\\infty} e^{-x}\\, \\textrm{d}x &= \\lim_{a \\to \\infty} \\int_{0}^{a} e^{-x}\\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\ &= \\lim_{a \\to \\infty} \\left[-e^{-x}\\right]_0^a \\\\ &= -\\lim_{a \\to \\infty} \\left({e^{-a}}-1\\right) \\\\ &= 1. \\end{aligned}$\nSo the integral converges, and statement I is true.\n- Computing the integral in statement II, we get\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{- \\infty}^{0}e^{-x}dx= \\lim_{a \\to - \\infty}\\int_{a}^{0}e^{-x}dx = \\lim_{a \\to - \\infty}[- e^{-x}]_{a}^{0} = \\lim_{a \\to - \\infty}(e^{-a} - 1) = + \\infty$.\nSo the integral diverges, and statement II is true.\n- The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty f(x) \\, \\textrm dx= \\int_{-\\infty}^0 f(x) \\, \\textrm dx + \\int_0^\\infty f(x) \\, \\textrm dx$.\nHowever, the first integral on the right-hand side is divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent, and statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, statements I, II, and III are all true."] [17592186105530 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x$."] [17592186105530 :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x + \\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\int_{-\\infty}^0 \\dfrac {1}{x^2+1} \\,\\textrm{d}x &= \\lim_{a \\to -\\infty} \\int_{a}^0 \\dfrac {1}{x^2+1} \\,\\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt] & = \\lim_{a \\to -\\infty}\\left(\\arctan (x) \\Big \\mid _a^0 \\right) \\\\[5pt] & = \\lim_{a \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan \\left(0 \\right) - \\arctan \\left(a \\right) \\right] \\\\[5pt] & = 0 - \\lim_{a \\to -\\infty} \\arctan \\left(a \\right) \\\\[5pt] &=-\\left(-\\dfrac \\pi 2 \\right) \\\\[5pt] & = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function (i.e., it is symmetrical about the $y$-axis). This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^\\infty \\dfrac {1}{x^2+1} \\,\\textrm{d}x =\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x &= \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x + \\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}+ \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\pi. \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186105532 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch a graph of the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1382/Source/Images/12de9a29b2f0e4eaad17166bc9fc3b49.png)\n\nIn this case, we must calculate the improper integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{9}{4x^2+1}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nAs usual, we break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{9}{4x^2+1}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} \\dfrac{9}{4x^2+1}\\,\\textrm{d}x + \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{9}{4x^2+1}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\int_{-\\infty}^0 \\dfrac {9}{4x^2+1} \\,\\textrm{d}x & = 9\\lim_{a \\to -\\infty}\\left(\\left[\\dfrac{1}{2}\\arctan \\left(2x \\right) \\right]_a^0 \\right) \\\\[5pt] & = \\dfrac{9}{2} \\lim_{a \\to -\\infty} \\left[\\arctan \\left(0 \\right) - \\arctan \\left(2a \\right) \\right] \\\\[5pt] & = 0 - \\dfrac{9}{2}\\lim_{a \\to -\\infty} \\arctan \\left(2a \\right) \\\\[5pt] & = -\\dfrac{9}{2} \\left(-\\dfrac \\pi 2 \\right) \\\\[5pt] & = \\dfrac{9\\pi}{4}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. Therefore, we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^\\infty\\dfrac {9}{4x^2+1} \\,\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{9\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{9}{4x^2+1}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{9\\pi}{4}+ \\dfrac{9\\pi}{4}= \\dfrac{9\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, the required area is equal to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nsquare units."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186104346 17592186104347 17592186104348 17592186104349 17592186104350 17592186105036 17592186105037 17592186105038 17592186105039 17592186105040] "immutable_fields" [[17592186104345 :answer-field/correct] [17592186104345 :answer-field/id] [17592186104345 :answer-field/key] [17592186104345 :answer-field/type] [17592186105035 :answer-field/correct] [17592186105035 :answer-field/id] [17592186105035 :answer-field/key] [17592186105035 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
